docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:126:Reject all unlisted nodes, including assignments/declarations, callbacks, loops, dynamic member access, prototype/constructor access, imports, process/filesystem APIs, arbitrary evaluation, `page.evaluate`, browser/context management, and unsupported TypeScript constructs. Bound AST depth, statements, selector lengths, and regex forms; unsafe regex constructs are rejected rather than executing arbitrary patterns.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:130:**Text-entry role equivalence (executor 2.1.0, VAL-031).** For `fill` and `press`, a terminal `getByRole` with role `textbox`, `searchbox` or `combobox` (options limited to `name`/`exact`/`disabled`) means "the text-entry control with that accessible name". The authored role is always tried first and wins whenever it matches. Only after it has matched nothing for `min(2 s, timeout/4)` does the runner try the other two roles with the same options, and it acts only when exactly one element matches. Several matches are refused, and the runner never picks the first one. Every use is logged to the operator. This is a versioned, documented executor semantic, not a silent reinterpretation: an artifact that resolves on its authored role behaves exactly as under 2.0.0, with one exception: visible-only resolution, described next. See `studio-runner/README.md`.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:132:**Visible-only resolution of actions (executor 2.1.0, VAL-040).** For the action operations (`click`, `fill`, `press`, `check`, `uncheck`, `hover`, `selectOption`), the runner narrows the locator to its visible matches (`filter({ visible: true })`) before applying strict mode. Hidden elements can't be acted on, so they no longer make a step ambiguous. Two or more visible matches still fail with the strict-mode message, where N is the visible count. Exactly one is acted on. If none is visible by the deadline, the step fails as "not visible", or as "no element matched" when nothing matched at all. The runner never uses `.first()`. Assertions resolve the unfiltered locator and are unchanged. `force: true` opts out. Under text-entry equivalence, the same filter applies to the authored role and to the alternatives. Each time the filter changes the outcome, the runner logs one operator line, `visible-only-resolution: step=<i> total=<n>`.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:144:Show the original step, a readable action/target/assertion summary derived from validated operations, and expandable executable source. Unsupported/ambiguous instructions receive actionable diagnostics; observed execution/video verifies the proposed interpretation.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:146:Gateway outage saves the draft and leaves compilation `STALE` with an error. Exhausted repair yields `NEEDS_REVISION`. Recovery of an abandoned compile request marks it retryable without replacing the confirmed history. All gateway calls carry audit metadata (test, CSI, user, model/prompt versions, latency, outcome), never resolved values.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:153:2. In one transaction, snapshot the confirmed revision, CSI, environment, nonsecret configuration, principal and references; insert `PENDING` history plus its globally unique dispatch ledger row. Persist idempotency-key/request-digest mapping here, scoped to principal and route, so retry across API replicas returns the same execution. Existing in-memory idempotency middleware alone is insufficient. Return 202 only after commit.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:155:4. Persist an attempt ID and `DISPATCHING` state before the HTTP send. `POST /v1/runs` is a short admission request. The runner reserves capacity and performs an authenticated admission CAS through the API before launching Chromium. Only one `(runnerInstanceId, admissionId)` can own an attempt; duplicate identical admission is idempotent and competing admission gets 409. A failed admission handshake never starts a browser.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:158:Runner 429 means definite nonacceptance: no durable admission and no browser started. Retry it with `Retry-After`/jitter within the pending deadline. Timeouts, connection loss, or 5xx are ambiguous: consult admission/progress/manifest state and await recovery; do not resend browser work. Disable automatic POST retries in clients and proxies. Expired leases may reclaim work only when no dispatch attempt was issued; a dispatcher crash between recording an attempt and sending it may intentionally end as unknown rather than risk duplicate effects.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:162:Validate request/artifact → acquire slot → admission handshake → launch Chromium → create one context/page with native recording → report browser start → execute permitted operations with step-start/finish reporting. Default step timeout is 30 seconds and total browser runtime 120 seconds, configurable within server limits. Stop on the first step failure; remaining steps become `SKIPPED` with null timings. Unexpected popups are closed and reported as unsupported rather than silently continuing on another page.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:194:Send heartbeat comments every 15 seconds. Use an explicit bounded stream lifetime (initially 60 seconds) and reconnect to refresh identity/CSI authorization. Set `Cache-Control: no-store`; disable buffering through the actual gateway/ingress; idle timeouts must exceed heartbeat cadence. Native EventSource uses the approved session-cookie route; a bearer-only platform requires a header-capable client inside the centralized API service. No credential query parameters. Verify cross-origin cookie/CORS behavior where applicable.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:196:Use bounded dedicated senders and coalesced per-client pending snapshots; slow clients must not block callbacks, DB readers, or ordinary check workers. Clean up on error/timeout/completion and component unmount. One subscription per actively viewed execution. Close explicitly on `streamComplete=true`, after evidence is settled. On persistent transport failure use the authorized GET snapshot with bounded polling; distinguish 401/403 via the normal client and stop rather than falling back around denied access.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:204:Record native webm on the single page's context. Await context closure to finalize the file; sudden process death may leave no playable recording. Explicitly configure video resolution so the text needed for evidence remains readable. [Playwright video documentation](https://playwright.dev/docs/videos) describes context finalization and per-page recordings.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:206:Keep outcome (`PASSED | FAILED | ERROR`) separate from evidence: `PROCESSING | AVAILABLE | FAILED | UNAVAILABLE | EXPIRED`. Upload failure never overwrites a known assertion outcome. `UNAVAILABLE` covers runs without a usable recording; `FAILED` covers evidence production/upload failures. Expose `videoError`, `videoExpiresAt`, and whether an available recording is partial. `streamComplete` means run processing/evidence delivery is settled, not that retention can no longer change availability.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:221:An authenticated ambient session/gateway route is required for `<video>` and download requests; same-origin alone does not supply a JavaScript bearer header. This is a staging prerequisite shared with SSE. Presigned redirects are post-V1, contingent on desktop reachability and access policy.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:225:For each attempted step persist `offsetMs` (start on recording timeline) and `durationMs`. Use a monotonic runner clock and calibrate its origin against the pinned recorder's first frames. V1 acceptance target: seek within 500 ms of the visible step start on the controlled fixture, including initial navigation and failure cases; validate this target in staging before claiming it for real applications. Clicking a wait/assertion seeks its start, not necessarily the later instant its condition becomes true. `SKIPPED` steps have null timing and disabled seek. No synthetic index-times-ten mapping remains.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:233:V1 owns Studio history and last-run summaries by CSI. The association between `UI_TEST` checks and Studio definitions, application/component metadata, and unified dashboard/report projections must be specified in the later integration story; updating a Studio row alone does not populate those views. Preserve optional `checkId` in history for that integration. Scheduling and unified reporting controls must not imply that an unsupported path is operational in V1.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:246:| Dispatch ledger (new, unpartitioned) | Globally unique `execution_id`; history partition locator; persistent execute-idempotency key/digest; attempt and admission identities; claim lease/state/deadlines; last runner sequence and API snapshot revision; deterministic manifest key |
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:256:- The current table has `(id, started_at)` as its primary key. Ledger `execution_id` is globally unique and carries the partition locator; repositories must honor the physical composite key for update/recovery. Do not introduce an invalid single-column foreign key to the partitioned table.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:258:- Widen `ui_tests.last_run_status` to include `ERROR`; update last-run projection only when the finishing execution is newer by `(acceptedAt, executionId)` than the projected one. An older late completion must not overwrite a newer run.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:260:Per-step states: `PENDING | RUNNING | PASS | FAIL | SKIPPED`. Return one result entry per original step. Skipped/unstarted timings are null. `PENDING` is accepted but not yet browser-started; `RUNNING` means an authenticated runner-start report was committed. Timeouts are `ERROR` with a code, not a distinct execution status. A step locator/assertion timeout is `FAIL` and execution `FAILED`; a parent-enforced total timeout is `ERROR / TIMEOUT`.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:277:Freeze one OpenAPI/JSON-schema contract and MSW fixtures in story 10.1 before wiring frontend/backend. Use `steps: string[]`, typed `variables` (`plain` or `secretRef`), `configuration: {browser, viewport, timeoutSeconds, waitStrategy}`, and `environment` in test requests. The server generates structured artifacts; clients never submit executable code as authoritative.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:279:Canonical browser value is `chromium`. A migration adapter accepts legacy `chrome` and normalizes it; other engines reject before execution. Migrate the DB browser constraint/current rows explicitly. Convert legacy structured `TestIR` to ordered `originalText`, JSON strings to typed DTO fields, flattened viewport fields to `configuration.viewport`, and legacy `COMPLETED/TIMEOUT` to the canonical outcome contract. Remove client grammar validation and Python generation as authoritative paths. Include schedule/status editing only when that capability ships.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:291:| Runner `GET /health`, `GET /ready` | Liveness and ability to admit safely; capacity saturation alone keeps readiness true and returns 429. Draining sets readiness false |
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:299:Use deployment configuration for the R2D2 gateway endpoint (Vertex SDK base URL, project and location), runner/callback URLs, service credentials, Sonic endpoint/bucket/path-style access and the execution kill switch. Per-component concurrency, payload and retention limits are specified in §§4–6; keep their implementation values in one configuration source.
docs/architecture/studio-execution.md:315:References persist in authoring/artifacts; resolved values exist only briefly in worker/runner/browser memory and the intended target request. Never persist them in jobs, retry queues, manifests or diagnostics. Runner scrubs known values and tested encoded variants from every outbound diagnostic before persistence. Scope secret use to approved origins and action arguments. Secret reference names alone grant no permission.

